  

<h2 id="because-a-document-that-lives-in-its-own-link-is-a-first-and-rough-planning-needs-a-distraction-free-space">Because a document that lives in its own link is a first, and rough planning needs a distraction-free space</h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/9969344b-8f3f-4201-9db9-fcbd191c500e/pasted-percent20image-percent2020261102083814.png" title="Screenshot of the calendar page on the tinylittleoffice website, showing a popup for filling in event details" alt="Screenshot of the calendar page on the tinylittleoffice website, showing a popup for filling in event details" width="2932" height="1598"></p>
<p>*This post can also be read on <a href="https://medium.com/@miscellaneplans/ptpl-231-where-tiny-little-office-is-big-on-cuteness-and-3-questions-about-paper-vs-digital-3c36335b6514" target="_blank">Medium</a>. It helps to support me when Medium members read it there.)</p>
<h3 id="productivity-inspiration">Productivity inspiration</h3>
<p><a href="https://tinylittleoffice.com" target="_blank">Tiny Little Office</a> is a free online office suite. Nothing is saved unless you choose to save it. The documents you create are easy to share, and the link to each document <em>is the document itself</em>. Yeah, I find that hard to wrap my head around, too!</p>
<p>From their <a href="https://github.com/aboard-io/tinylittleoffice" target="_blank">GitHub page</a>:</p>
<ul>
<li>A letter, a spreadsheet, a presentation or a calendar is held in the address of its page<br>
</li>
<li>Copy the link and you have copied the document<br>
</li>
<li>Whoever has the link has the document.<br>
</li>
<li>There is no account.</li>
</ul>
<p>I’m pleased to see you can download the documents in various formats, including plain text, Markdown, <span class="small-caps">PDF</span>, and even iCalendar (<span class="small-caps">ICS</span>).</p>
<p>It’s super cute and has been fun to play with. I appreciate the concept. It would be good for the quick sharing of documents and ideas without accounts or downloads. Absolutely not meant for collaboration, and only designed for small and simple things. Let me know if you find a practical use for it in your own digital world.</p>
<h3 id="adventures-in-plain-text-and-a-little-paper">Adventures in plain text, and a little paper</h3>
<p>This week I’ve been thinking about planning. The initial ideas you write down when looking at the week ahead, or when a project comes to mind and you want to brain dump it before setting structures and deadlines in place.</p>
<p>Question: Where is the best place for rough planning? Paper, or digital?</p>
<p>Some people, like Carl Pullein, whose courses I am going through at the moment, strongly recommend using a physical notebook. Other people in the productivity sphere use their notes app of choice, or a simple text file. I’ve had paper phases and plain text phases for planning, and continue to enjoy cycling between them.</p>
<p>The argument for paper cites the (unavoidable, they say) distraction potential of digital devices.</p>
<p>Those in favour of devices talk of convenience, efficiency, and Airplane Mode.</p>
<p>This kind of<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>which is best?” question appears to be solely applicable to those who have the visual and motor capabilities to choose between the two. But as I’ve written previously, <a href="https://ellanew.com/2025/05/26/ptpl-158-simple-tools-give-relief-from-overwhelm-2" class="wikilink">it’s possible to approximate the benefits of analog on digital devices</a> when dealing with accessibility issues.</p>
<p>The answer is therefore to make your initial plans using whatever media you can comfortably use, that best gives you a distraction and interruption-free space to get all your thoughts down. Use your intuition, along with what you have observed about yourself and your propensity for distraction around particular tools and environments.</p>
<p>I’ll be using paper for rough plans and text files to make sense of them. This is what I’m contemplating as the new year approaches:</p>
<ul>
<li>Are plain text files sufficient for all kinds of planning, including brain dump style initial thoughts?<br>
</li>
<li><a href="https://ellanew.com/2025/09/01/ptpl-171-edc-one-page-notebook" class="wikilink">1-P</a>, separate notebook, or keep everything in one universal book with all my other notes?<br>
</li>
<li>Is an <span class="small-caps">A4</span> page necessary, or is <span class="small-caps">A6</span> spread (<span class="small-caps">A5</span> equivalent) big enough for rough planning without feeling restrictive?</li>
</ul>
